Hyperparathyroidism
Endocrine & Metabolic Disorders
Bone pain/fractures, Renal colic, Elevated calcium with normal/high PTH, Lethargy/constipation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: EMD-018
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting, severe left flank pain radiating to the groin, accompanied by severe constipation and diffuse bone aches.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified acute, severe flank pain consistent with renal colic, unmanageable with oral analgesia, in a patient with progressive systemic symptoms. Immediate out-of-hours assessment was required to administer potent parenteral analgesia and rule out an obstructed, infected renal tract before morning clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The pain in my left side is agonizing, I'm vomiting from it, and my whole body aches down to the bones."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the abrupt onset of severe, colicky left flank pain radiating to the left groin over the past 6 hours, accompanied by nausea and vomiting. The pain is rated 10/10 and prevents the patient from remaining still. Upon deeper questioning regarding a longer timeline, the patient reports a six-month history of diffuse bone pain, generalized muscle weakness, and severe, unremitting constipation. They also note severe lethargy, profound polyuria, and excessive thirst.
Functional Status: Currently pacing and writhing in agony; unable to sit still or find a comfortable position.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone, increasing risk of dehydration from recurrent vomiting.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/95 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 98 percent on room air | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Highly distressed, diaphoretic, pacing the room holding the left flank.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals extreme left costovertebral angle tenderness and left lower quadrant tenderness on deep palpation, without rebound or guarding. Bowel sounds are diminished. Musculoskeletal exam reveals diffuse tenderness on palpation over the anterior tibiae and forearms. Ocular slit-lamp examination is unavailable, but gross inspection is clear. Diagnostic tetrad features observed: Bone pain (diffuse tenderness), Renal colic (acute left flank pain), Lethargy/constipation, and anticipated Elevated calcium with normal/high PTH pending formal metabolic panel.
Environmental Observations: Evidence of multiple over-the-counter laxatives and analgesics failing to provide relief.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular prescribed medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Renal Colic secondary to suspected Primary Hyperparathyroidism - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute pyelonephritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of fever and chills, and the highly colicky nature of the pain causing the patient to pace, rather than lie still.

Musculoskeletal back pain - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The severe radiation to the groin, the costovertebral angle tenderness, and the presence of concurrent systemic symptoms (constipation, bone pain).

Abdominal aortic aneurysm leak - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal femoral pulses, absence of a pulsatile abdominal mass, and the classic colicky presentation in a stable, normotensive patient.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute episode of severe renal colic, superimposed on a chronic syndrome of bone pain, constipation, and polyuria. Triage accurately flagged the unmanageable pain and vomiting. Objective examination confirmed left-sided urolithiasis signs. While the renal colic requires acute management, the systemic constellation heavily indicates underlying primary hyperparathyroidism driving hypercalciuria. Delaying care forced the patient to endure excruciating pain and risked severe dehydration. Immediate out-of-hours evaluation was clinically mandated to provide potent parenteral analgesia, manage vomiting, and coordinate an urgent urological/endocrinological workup, fully satisfying urgent MBS criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular ketorolac 30mg and intramuscular prochlorperazine 12.5mg for acute pain and nausea control. Arranged urgent outpatient CT KUB (Kidneys, Ureters, Bladder) and a comprehensive metabolic panel including ionized calcium and intact PTH for the following morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: By continuously sipping water to achieve 3 liters of intake per day (action), you maintain a high volume of urine flow through the kidneys (cause), which physically flushes out small stones and prevents calcium from settling and forming new ones (effect).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Nil.
DME/Safety (Optional): Urine strainer provided to catch the stone for laboratory analysis.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 24 hours to review CT results and blood tests, and arrange urology/endocrinology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops a fever above 38 C, experiences sudden uncontrollable shaking chills, or becomes completely unable to pass any urine, present immediately to the nearest emergency department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The acute pain is a kidney stone, but the combination of your long-term symptoms suggests an underlying overactive parathyroid gland causing too much calcium in your blood. Blood tests are required to confirm this.
Clinical Course: The acute pain will fluctuate until the stone passes. If the underlying calcium issue is confirmed, surgery to remove the overactive gland is typically required to stop recurrent stones and bone damage.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Use the provided urine strainer every time you void. Do not take any further anti-inflammatories today as you have received a high-dose injection. Maintain high fluid intake.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The injection directly reduces the severe inflammation and spasm in the tube connecting your kidney to your bladder. The broader symptoms suggest your body is pulling calcium out of your bones and dumping it into your kidneys, forming these stones.
Actionable Steps: Strain all urine. Drink large amounts of water immediately. Attend the radiology clinic first thing in the morning for the urgent scan.

